{"id":"dbfdf05f-b3dd-47e5-920a-fd1500fa8845","object":"chat.completion","created":1784832401,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import collections\\n    payload = case.get('payload', {})\\n    nodes = payload.get('nodes')\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list):\\n        raise ValueError('Missing or invalid nodes')\\n    req_syms = payload.get('required_symbols', [])\\n    if not isinstance(req_syms, list):\\n        raise ValueError('required_symbols must be a list')\\n    # Build maps\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    children_by_parent = collections.defaultdict(list)\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n.get('id')\\n        parent = n.get('parent')\\n        symbol = n.get('symbol')\\n        depth = n.get('depth')\\n        removable = n.get('removable')\\n        if nid is None or parent is None and nid != 'root':\\n            raise ValueError('Node missing id or parent')\\n        if symbol is None or depth is None or removable is None:\\n            raise ValueError('Node missing required fields')\\n        node_by_id[nid] = n\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children_by_parent[parent].append(nid)\\n    # Check root exists\\n    if 'root' not in node_by_id or node_by_id['root']['parent'] is not None:\\n        raise ValueError('Root not found or misconfigured')\\n    # Check required symbols initially satisfied\\n    sym_present = {s: False for s in req_syms}\\n    for n in node_by_id.values():\\n        if n['symbol'] in sym_present:\\n            sym_present[n['symbol']] = True\\n    if not all(sym_present.values()):\\n        raise ValueError('Required symbol missing initially')\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    removal_order = []\\n    # Helper to check required symbols after removal of a candidate\\n    def symbol_still_covered(cand_id):\\n        # Count retained nodes per required symbol excluding candidate\\n        counts = {s: 0 for s in req_syms}\\n        for nid in retained:\\n            if nid == cand_id:\\n                continue\\n            sym = node_by_id[nid]['symbol']\\n            if sym in counts:\\n                counts[sym] += 1\\n        return all(v > 0 for v in counts.values())\\n    while True:\\n        candidates = []\\n        for nid in retained:\\n            node = node_by_id[nid]\\n            if not node['removable']:\\n                continue\\n            # Check no retained children\\n            has_retained_child = any(c in retained for c in children_by_parent[nid])\\n            if has_retained_child:\\n                continue\\n            # Check required symbols after removal\\n            if not symbol_still_covered(nid):\\n                continue\\n            candidates.append(nid)\\n        if not candidates:\\n            break\\n        # Deepest first, then smallest id\\n        def sort_key(nid):\\n            return (-node_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid)\\n        chosen = min(candidates, key=sort_key)\\n        retained.remove(chosen)\\n        removal_order.append(chosen)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'retained_ids': retained_ids,\\n        'removal_order': removal_order\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` that performs grammar-guided node reduction. The problem is about reducing a parse tree (nodes with parent, depth, etc.) while preserving required symbols and some notion of \"interestingness\" (oracle). The description mentions \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\" but simplified. We must follow a bounded objective: reduce tree by removing removable nodes (subject to constraints), with tie-breaking by depth (deeper first), and node ID for equal depth. Must preserve that all required symbols remain present. Also, we cannot remove a node if it has retained children (i.e., if we remove a node, its children must also be removed or reattached? Actually the description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we cannot remove a node if any of its descendants are retained. That implies we should consider removing leaf nodes first, or at least nodes whose entire subtree is being removed. But also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we need to check that after removal, all required symbols still appear somewhere in the tree.\n\nThe process: We have a list of nodes with parent references, depth, removable flag, symbol, utility. The root is depth 0, removable false, symbol \"root\". We need to produce a list of retained node IDs (in some order? The spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we need to output two lists: retained_ids (sorted), and removal_order (the order in which nodes were removed). Possibly also other metrics? The problem statement: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So the solve function should return a dict with keys \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\".\n\nWe need to implement an algorithm: start with all nodes, then iteratively consider removing removable nodes (that are not required symbol nodes, not root) in order of descending depth (deeper first), and for equal depth, lower node ID (alphabetical? but IDs are strings, we can compare lexicographically). For each candidate, check if removing it (i.e., not retaining it) breaks any constraint: (1) node has no retained children? Actually \"node with retained children\" means if the node has any child that is still retained, we cannot remove it. Since we are considering removing nodes, we need to ensure that all children of this node are already removed (or will be removed simultaneously? But iterative removal means we remove nodes one by one, so we can only remove a node if it has no retained children at that moment. So we need to process from leaves upward. That matches deeper first. Also check that required symbols still appear after removal (i.e., at least one node with each required symbol remains). If those conditions hold, we mark the node as removed, and record it. Continue until no more removable nodes can be removed.\n\nAt the end, retained_ids are those not removed. Sort them (presumably by ID). Return the removal order as list.\n\nWe must not mutate the input case. Build a copy of node dicts or work with indices.\n\nImplementation approach: Build a mapping from node ID to node dict. Also build a mapping from parent ID to list of children IDs. Then we can compute depth, but depth is already given. Use depth for ordering.\n\nWe need to maintain a set of retained node IDs. Initially all nodes are retained. Then we maintain a list of removable candidates: nodes that are removable (flag True) and not root and not required symbol? Actually required symbols are a list of symbols, not node IDs. A node with a symbol that is in required_symbols cannot be removed? The spec says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, it cannot be removed. However, note that there could be multiple nodes with the same symbol; we only need at least one of each required symbol to remain. So we can remove a node even if its symbol is required, as long as another node with that symbol remains. But the spec says \"required symbol\" - it might mean any node that matches that symbol? Actually \"required_symbols\" is a list of symbols that must appear in the final tree. So each symbol in that list must be present in at least one retained node. So we cannot remove all nodes that carry that symbol. But we can remove some of them if at least one remains. However, the spec says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" - ambiguous. Let's read carefully: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" I think \"required symbol\" refers to a node whose symbol is in the required list, i.e., that specific node is considered \"required\" (maybe because it's the only occurrence). But then they also say \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That suggests we can remove a node if after removal, all required symbols are still represented (by other nodes). So the \"required symbol\" prohibition is not a blanket ban on any node with that symbol; it's a ban on removing a node that is the sole representative of that symbol. But the phrasing \"a required symbol\" might mean a node that is marked as required by the grammar? Actually there is a field \"removable\" which is false for root and some others. The required_symbols list is separate. So I think \"required symbol\" here means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. But then the second condition \"only if all required symbols remain represented\" allows removal if they are still represented. So the first statement \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" is too strong; it's likely a misinterpretation. Let's look at the context: The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" But later \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This seems contradictory unless \"required symbol\" means something else. Possibly \"required symbol\" refers to the node that is the root of a subtree that is required? Hmm.\n\nGiven the typical Perses algorithm, you can remove any node as long as the resulting tree is still valid (grammar-valid) and interestingness-preserved. Here grammar validity is simplified to just symbol presence? Actually they mention \"grammar_valid\" as a property. But in this simplified version, we don't have a grammar; we only have nodes with symbols, parent, and required symbols. The problem says \"Grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but we don't have replacements. It's just deletion. So \"grammar_valid\" likely means that the tree structure (parent-child relationships) must remain valid after removal. Removing a node should remove its entire subtree (since we cannot reattach children). The condition \"node with retained children\" prevents removing a node that has children still in the tree, because those children would become orphaned. So we must remove leaves first. That is standard.\n\nThus, the algorithm is: repeatedly remove a leaf (removable and not root) that is not the last node with any required symbol, in order of depth descending, and for equal depth by node ID ascending (since stable tie break, we want deterministic order). The \"removal order\" is the sequence of node IDs as we remove them.\n\nBut what about nodes that are not leaves but have all children already removed? That is a leaf at that point. So we can treat it as leaf after children are removed.\n\nSo we maintain a set of retained nodes. We consider removing nodes that have no retained children (i.e., are leaves in the current retained tree). Among those, we filter by removable=True and not root and not the last node of a required symbol (i.e., after removal, for each required symbol, at least one retained node still has that symbol). Then we pick the one with greatest depth (max depth), and if tie, smallest node ID (lexicographically). Remove it (remove from retained set), add to removal order. Repeat until no further node can be removed.\n\nAt the end, retained_ids sorted (lexicographically) and removal_order.\n\nWe need to be careful: depth is given; we can use that.\n\nImplementation steps in solve(case):\n\n- Extract nodes list from case payload.\n- Build dict: node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n- Build children dict: children_by_parent = defaultdict(list). For each node (except root with parent null), add child id to parent's list.\n- Initialize retained_set = set of all node ids.\n- Initialize removal_order = [].\n- Initialize required_symbols_set = set(case['payload']['required_symbols']) (includes 'root'? Usually root is always there, but it's in required_symbols? In fixtures, required_symbols includes 'root' and 's1').\n- While True:\n    - Find candidates: for each node id in retained_set:\n        - node = node_by_id[id]\n        - If not node['removable']: continue (includes root as removable false)\n        - If id == 'root': continue (but root is not removable anyway)\n        - Check if node has any retained children: any child id in children_by_parent[id] that is in retained_set? If yes, skip.\n        - Check if after removal, all required symbols still represented: For each sym in required_symbols_set, check if there exists some retained node (other than this one) that has that symbol. That is, we need to ensure for each sym, the count of retained nodes with that symbol after removal is >=1. We can precompute symbol counts. But simpler: Iterate over required symbols, see if there is at least one retained node with that symbol not being this node. However, if this node is the only one with a required symbol, then removal would violate. So we can compute symbol counts per retained set. But doing it for each candidate each iteration is O(N*R) which is fine for small N. We'll do that.\n    - If no candidates, break.\n    - Choose candidate: max depth, then min id (lexicographic). Depth is node['depth'].\n    - Remove the chosen id from retained_set, append to removal_order.\n- After loop, retained_ids = sorted(list(retained_set)).\n- Return {\"retained_ids\": retained_ids, \"removal_order\": removal_order}.\n\nTesting with the provided fixtures: They have 7 nodes. Let's simulate for first case to see if algorithm yields expected? We don't have expected output, but we can reason. The required symbols are root and s1. root is depth 0 not removable. s1 appears in n1 (depth1, not removable) and n4 (depth2, removable). So at least one s1 must remain. n1 is not removable, so it will stay. Therefore we can remove n4. Other removable nodes: n2 (depth1, removable), n6 (depth2, removable). n2 is parent of n4; if we remove n4 first, n2 becomes leaf? Actually n2 has child n4, but n4 might be removed, then n2 has no retained children, so n2 becomes removable. n6 is child of n1 (not removable) so n6 is leaf initially. n5 is not removable. So order: deep first: depth2 nodes: n4, n6. Both depth2. Which has smaller ID? n4 vs n6: 'n4' < 'n6', so n4 first. Remove n4. Then candidates: n2 (now leaf) depth1, n6 depth2. Depth2 n6 removed next. Then n2 depth1, maybe also n3? n3 is not removable. So remove n2. Then no more removable nodes (n1 not removable, root not). So removal order: n4, n6, n2. Retained: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted: n1, n3, n5, root. That seems plausible.\n\nBut need to also check condition \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" In this case, after removing n4, we still have n1 with s1, so ok. After removing n6, s0 still present? s0 is not required, so ok. After removing n2, all required symbols still present.\n\nThus algorithm seems correct.\n\nOne nuance: What if required symbols include a symbol that appears only on a removable node, and that node is not leaf? Then we cannot remove it until after its children are removed? Actually we can only remove leaf nodes. So if that node has children, we must remove all children first, then we might remove it. But then the required symbol would be lost after removing it, so we cannot remove it. So it stays. That's fine.\n\nEdge case: If there is a node that is required symbol and leaf, but there is another node with same symbol, we can remove it. So our check works.\n\nAlso note: The root node has parent null and depth 0, removable false, symbol root. It is always retained.\n\nImplementation details: Use Python's standard library only. JSON parsing is not needed as input is already dict. We are to write solve(case) where case is a dict with structure as given. Return dict.\n\nWe must not mutate input: we are only reading.\n\nRaise ValueError for infeasible? The instruction says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So if case is malformed (missing fields, wrong types) we raise ValueError. Also if there is no possible reduction? That's not infeasible; we just return empty removal order. Infeasible might be if required symbols cannot be satisfied initially? But they are given; we can check that initially every required symbol appears in at least one node. If not, raise ValueError. Also check that root exists, etc.\n\nSimplify: we'll assume input is valid per fixture; but we can do basic validation: check that nodes list is present, each node has required fields, root with parent null, etc. For brevity, we might skip extensive validation, but the spec says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So we should check that each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also that required_symbols is a list. Also that there is at least one node with parent null (root). If any missing, raise ValueError. But we can assume it's correct for the test cases. However, to be safe, we'll do simple validation.\n\nNow produce JSON output with implementation key.\n\nWe'll write the Python code inside a string. Ensure proper escaping.\n\nWe'll also include comments? Not necessary but can be inside code. The JSON string must be valid.\n\nLet's craft the solution."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1852,"completion_tokens":3848,"total_tokens":5700,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3092},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":60},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}